{"seq":60,"ts":"2026-11-01T14:52:49.302Z","kind":"event","event":{"type":"MESSAGE_PUBLISHED","messageId":"T-1","boundCandidateSha":"81ea62951924d2f75034f965ac5a48b444cebfb9","boundContractVersion":{"snapshot":1,"sectionSha256":"8f1dc0d3a59e7b0713ee0f57327eed1452cc7e2ab0cabc3eadb5b774a754a445"},"boundRecordVersion":1,"content":{"type":"tradeoff","title":"Export subtract in the same module.exports object literal as sum","summary":"The candidate adds subtract as a standalone function and exports it inside the existing module.exports = { sum, subtract } literal, so a single destructuring require still yields both functions. This mirrors how sum is exposed and keeps subtract reachable from './sum.js' with no new export surface.","context":"The stated entry point is that consumers require subtract the same way as sum; the alternative would be a second module.exports assignment or a separate export statement, which fragments the module's public surface.","evidence":["sum.js:9 module.exports = { sum, subtract }","sum.js:5-7 function subtract(a, b) { return a - b; }","test.js:3 const { sum, subtract } = require('./sum.js')"],"importance":"medium"}}}
